=== REASONING ===

D1. Canned replies are keyed by GOLDEN NAME, not by sha256(prompt).
    The prompt's D7.1 sketches sha256-keyed canonicalization, but a
    hash-keyed cache only pays off once we have a recording pass that
    *populated* the hashes. F6 ships the harness; the recording pass
    is intentionally deferred (record mode is a stub — see D6 below).
    Name-keying lets a human author golden + canned files together,
    review them in PR, and re-run them deterministically. The
    SequencedMock's per-golden FIFO contract is the deterministic
    contract that matters; sha256-routing is an orthogonal future
    enhancement that can land in U1+ alongside the recording pass.

D2. SequencedMock wraps Mock instead of editing it.
    The F6 allowed-files list names canned.go only — NOT mock.go.
    Editing mock.go to add a sequence field would silently break the
    "allowed files" contract. Instead canned.go declares
        type SequencedMock struct { *Mock; mu sync.Mutex; seq []CannedReply; idx int }
    and overrides Chat + Stream. Lookup precedence:
        1. FIFO sequence (per-golden, set via SetSequence)
        2. Embedded Mock's existing lookup (byHash → byPrompt → Default)
    The embedded Default still returns finish_reason=stop "ok" so a
    poorly-authored canned file that runs out of replies still
    terminates the dispatcher loop instead of looping forever.

D3. GenericStrategy adapts goldens.yaml feature headers.
    Real Strategy implementations (one per feature) land starting in
    U1. F6 doesn't have any Strategy implementations to test against
    yet, so the harness needs an adapter that:
      - reads system_prompt + tool list from the golden file's feature
        header,
      - returns BuiltPrompt = { Messages: [system: header.system_prompt],
        Tools: header.tools },
      - returns Context() = (nil, nil) so the runner pre-seeds the user
        message into StrategyInput.History without duplicating it.
    Once a feature ships its own Strategy in U1+, the runner will look
    up that registered Strategy (via a future hook) and skip the
    GenericStrategy adapter for that FeatureID.

D4. Stub tools satisfy the dispatcher whitelist.
    The dispatcher refuses to execute a tool whose name is not on
    BuiltPrompt.Tools. Goldens declare expected must_call_tools, so
    those tool names MUST be registered in the dispatcher's tool
    registry at run time. F6 registers no-op stub tools per goldens
    file: read-only stubs return {"ok":true,...}; mutating stubs
    (declared in the goldens header's mutating_tools list) get
    Mutates()=true and an auto-approve Confirm callback so the
    dispatcher's confirm gate doesn't block the run. This keeps the
    harness contract explicit: "if your golden mentions create_alert,
    create_alert is gated on a confirm-required path."

D5. Judge mode runs in nightly CI only.
    Per ADR-015 I4, the judge is the ONLY part of F6 that can make a
    real network call — and only because nightly CI explicitly opts
    in via JUDGE_PROVIDER + JUDGE_API_KEY env vars. The judge prompt
    template embeds seed=42 + temperature=0 (R6 mitigation) so two
    consecutive judge runs on the same answer produce identical
    scores. The judge wiring in cmd/ai-eval/main.go currently returns
    "judge provider not yet wired" — the harness scaffolding (template,
    parseJudgeReply, Result.JudgeScore) is in place; concrete provider
    plumbing lands when the first U1 feature needs it.

D6. Record mode is a stub in F6.
    `--record` exists as a flag; RunGolden in ModeRecord returns
    Pass=false with reason "record mode is not implemented in F6
    (canned files must be hand-authored)". The whole point of F6 is
    that PR/main CI is offline + deterministic. Adding a record path
    that hits a real provider in this slice would risk an accidental
    egress — and we don't yet have a feature whose canned files are
    too tedious to hand-author. The record pass + sha256-rerouting
    will land together when both are needed.

D7. ADR-015 invariants this slice touches: I3, I4, I10. See AI-OFF
    CONTRACT footer for the per-invariant evidence trail.

