=== AI-OFF CONTRACT ===

This is the ADR-015 footer. F6 touches three of the ten invariants;
the rest are listed N/A with a one-line justification each.

I1  Default-off feature flag.
    N/A — F6 does not introduce any new user-facing AI surface, just
    a developer/CI harness. No new ai_features.<id>.enabled flag.

I2  Per-feature provider binding.
    N/A — F6 does not register a new feature. The harness reads
    existing FeatureSpec data from goldens.yaml headers and runs them
    through the existing dispatcher.

I3  Baseline parity (every AI surface has a non-AI baseline).
    EVIDENCE: The harness is a developer-side tool, not a user-facing
    surface. It has no UI / no public route / no DB write path. It
    consumes Strategy + Dispatcher contracts but never bypasses them.
    No baseline endpoint, repo, handler, or migration is added,
    modified, or deleted in this slice. `git diff --stat HEAD --
    internal/ai/provider/mock/mock.go internal/ai/dispatch
    internal/ai/strategy internal/ai/features` is empty.

I4  Zero default egress.
    EVIDENCE:
      - Fast mode (`make ai-eval-fast` → `go run ./cmd/ai-eval --all`):
        builds mock.NewSequencedMock(mock.New(...)) — pure in-process
        provider with no http.Client, no socket dial, no DNS lookup.
        Verified above: 5/5 goldens pass with zero network calls.
      - Full mode: identical to fast except it emits a JUnit XML
        artifact via local file write.
      - Judged mode: opt-in only. Gated on JUDGE_PROVIDER +
        JUDGE_API_KEY env vars in CI; the workflow skips the job
        entirely if either secret is unset (see ai-eval.yml `if:` on
        the judged job). Local invocation of `make ai-eval-judged`
        without the env vars hits the cmd/ai-eval `buildJudgeProvider`
        branch which currently returns "judge provider not yet wired"
        — i.e. the egress path is documented as unwired in F6 and will
        not silently dial out.
      - Schema check tool: pure file walk + yaml.Unmarshal, no I/O
        beyond the local filesystem.

I5  Audit log every AI call.
    N/A — F6 does not invoke a real provider. The mock and the
    SequencedMock do not flow through the F3 ai_call_log code path
    (and shouldn't — their entire purpose is offline determinism).
    When the judged path lights up in nightly CI it WILL flow through
    the F3 code path of the underlying provider implementation,
    inheriting that audit guarantee for free.

I6  Confirm gate for mutating tools.
    EVIDENCE: stubRegistry in internal/ai/eval/tools.go marks every
    tool listed under FeatureSpec.MutatingTools as Mutates()=true. The
    runner provides an autoApproveConfirm callback so the dispatcher's
    confirm gate is exercised but not blocked. The gate is therefore
    preserved (the dispatcher still calls Confirm before Execute);
    the harness simply auto-approves so a goldens run can complete.
    Real user surfaces continue to require human confirmation per F4.

I7  Per-feature rate limits.
    N/A — F6 does not change the F1 rate-limit scaffolding. The mock
    is exempt by design (no remote call to throttle).

I8  PII redaction in audit log.
    N/A — same reason as I5.

I9  Provider abstraction (no direct SDK usage outside provider/).
    EVIDENCE: F6 imports zero LLM SDKs. The mock provider is
    in-process. The judge code path imports only internal/ai/provider
    interfaces, not any concrete SDK. `grep -R "github.com/openai\|
    github.com/anthropic\|cloud.google.com/go/vertexai" cmd/ai-eval
    internal/ai/eval tools/eval-schema-check` returns nothing.

I10 Per-feature isolation (no cross-feature shared state).
    EVIDENCE: every cmd/ai-eval flag, every eval.GoldenSet, every
    eval.Result, every report row is keyed by FeatureID. The runner
    builds a fresh stubRegistry, a fresh SequencedMock, and a fresh
    Dispatcher per golden — there is no shared mutable state across
    features or across goldens within a feature.

